Here I am trying to verify a property that my system (an ArrayList) is supposed to maintain: the index returned by the IndexOf and LastIndexOf methods is always valid. 
The test case should pass with any valid input:
If the element of the ArrayList is present in the ArrayList and then removes the element, the IndexOf and LastIndexOf should return a -1 which means the element has been removed.
If we try to add an element it should get the current count of the list, which indicates the index of the newly added element. 
It seems the original list is inconsistent before and after the call to the Contains method.
So if our method has to maintain this property what conditions should come under the PexAssume to make the test case pass?
I understand that there's no way to guarantee that Contains will always behave correctly since ArrayList is not deterministic, but what should be the condition under PexAssume to ensure the test case passes? 

A: The assumption should relate to the initialization of the ArrayList and the initial state of the elements it contains. This way, you ensure that the state of the system is valid at the beginning of the test and your code is only tested on valid data.
In your case, you want to assume that an ArrayList is created and added to it with any number of distinct elements, then you remove at least an element. The other aspects of any List should be checked for validity by the Pex engine and its observers. So the test case would look like this:
    [PexMethod]
    [PexAssumeValid]
    public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexTypicalValues]ArrayList arrList, [PexDistinctValues]int x)
    {
        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int Old_x = x;
        int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

        bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

        Assert.IsTrue((Old_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListCount && New_arrListIndexOfX == -1)
                    || (Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListCount && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1)
                    || (New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount && New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1)
                    || (New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount && New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1));
    }

This will ensure that your test case will work with different initial values for your list and your "removed" value x, and that you will be testing the system for validity at each step.
This will also ensure that your test case is not overly optimistic. If your assumptions about the state of your ArrayList didn't hold, the test could fail even though your system was internally consistent.
Also take note that there are different ways to write Pex tests and it's dependant on your specific requirements of what you want to test.

A: The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used on methods you want the Pex engine to run unit tests on. This allows the Pex engine to ensure that the inputs to methods are valid and consistent before the method runs.
You can use PexAssumeValid attribute to provide initial and final state information for your property under test.
Your condition under PexAssume should ensure that the list is created with at least one item and ensures the item to check is in the list.
For example:
    [PexMethod]
    [PexAssumeValid]
    public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexTypicalValues]ArrayList arrList, [PexDistinctValues]int x)
    {
       // code
    }

Where the [PexTypicalValues] generates typical values for the list and [PexDistinctValues] generates distinct values for x--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_Ret ? New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 : Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1);

         //an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is false, lastIndex of x before is more than or equal to after
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret ? New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 : Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);

         // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ Count stays the same if an addition happened in the array list
            Debug.Assert((Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount) || (Old_arrListCount + 1 == New_arrListCount));

        }
    }
}

This is just an example of how to use this testing method. It's an automated test, it is checking if the Contains function works correctly. It takes an array list and a value, checks if the list contains this value. If it does, Contains method should return true, otherwise it returns false, and the Count of the list should not change. Also, IndexOf and LastIndexOf should behave the same way.

A: ArrayList is considered obsolete and should not be used for new development. Consider using a List<T> instead, or a more specialized collection type like List<int> rather than ArrayList.
ArrayList has the potential to throw exceptions if used incorrectly, and the methods it exposes do not include virtual functions, so they cannot be overridden to provide custom behavior. It is also not strongly typed, which is a major drawback.
If you still need to use ArrayList, you should test it carefully because its use can cause runtime errors and problems when misused. You could write unit tests to verify that it works correctly in a variety of scenarios, including what would be considered "edge cases" or unusual usage scenarios.

EDIT: To clarify what I mean by testing, I would do something like this.
[TestMethod]
public void TestContains()
{
    ArrayList list = new ArrayList { 1, 2, 3 };
    Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(1)); // true
    Assert.IsFalse(list.Contains(4)); // false
}

[TestMethod]
public void TestCount()
{
    ArrayList list = new ArrayList { 1, 2, 3 };
    Assert.AreEqual(3, list.Count);
    list.Add(4);
    Assert.AreEqual(4, list.Count); // Count should increase
}

[TestMethod]
public void TestDuplicates()
{
    ArrayList list = new ArrayList { 1, 1, 2, 2 };
    Assert.AreEqual(2, list[0]); // Items should retain order
    Assert.AreEqual(2, list[2]);
}

This is an example of what the tests might look like. In the example, each test verifies a different aspect of ArrayList: it verifies the Contains method works as expected, the Count property works as expected, and it verifies that multiple copies of the same item are correctly handled.
Note: In real development, always choose the correct collection type from the System.Collections.Generic namespace. The ArrayList class was deprecated in .NET Framework 4.0 and it is recommended to stop its usage.

A: 
I am using a contract test automation tools, such as "TestDriven.Net" for my testing needs.

In this question, you are specifically asking for advice on how to perform a contract test using "TestDriven.Net" for an ArrayList class. However, the use of ArrayList is strongly discouraged due to its limitations that are pointed out by other posts. Moreover, TestDriven.Net is rather outdated when it comes to handling generics collections like List<T>.

Can you please guide me on how to perform a contract test using TestDriven .Net for the ArrayList class?

TestDriven.Net supports testing through unit tests, but does not natively support contract testing. Contract testing requires establishing preconditions and showing that operations maintain the contract after the operation has taken place. 

However, here is a basic example of how you would do a unit test:

Here is an example:

// [TestClass] attribute indicates that the following class is a test class
[TestClass]
public class ArrayListContractTest {

    // This is a simple unit test checking if ArrayList.Add adds an integer to the end of the ArrayList
    [TestMethod]
    public void TestAdd() {
        // Assume--------------------	    if (!New_Ret) { Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX); }
	    else { Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX); }
        }   
	}
}

This is the code of PUT_ContainsContract which ensures the 'Contains' method in ArrayList maintains the 'Count' and 'IndexOf' properties as consistent. PexObserve and Assert are used to assist in debugging and verifying the consistency.


Remember that your ArrayList under test should have a Contains method defined and properly implemented.

Also note that this might not catch all possible issues such as null reference exceptions, but it will help catch many common forms of inconsistent states. If you still see failures not caught by this test, you have more work to do.


A: This is not specifically an issue with your code, but an inherently random behavior of statistical testing environments like Pex.
Pex will generate test cases based on all possible paths and edge cases. It doesn't mean your code is 100% correct, it just means it has covered all branches of the code, whether it's right or not. While Pex is a strong tool, it shouldn't be used as the sole factor to measure the correctness of the code.
In most scenarios, you should aim for a higher code coverage. However, when it comes to branch coverage, it is often about ensuring that your code can handle all possible situations correctly. Therefore, the branches hit in Pex tests may not necessarily mean your implementation would fail in real usage scenarios. Hence, Pex doesn't substitute for regular code reviews, unit testing or integration testing that you should also have.
This whole discussion should be part of a larger design consideration where you make sure your implementation covers all edge cases and it can handle any unexpected inputs.
But in your specific question regarding ArrayList, a Contains method, the code coverage and Pex test will both handle this scenario:
Pex will try many inputs based on possible paths through the logic in your code. It may hit all branches but this does not necessarily mean that the code works correctly for all possible inputs. For ArrayList's Contains method, it seems like the current coverage suffices as all possible inputs have been tried by the testing tool. Any possible inputs beyond this are probably untested and would not be caught by the testing tool since it is merely simulating user interaction with your program and generating many different types of input. 
Keep in mind that these tools are only guidelines rather than rules, and testing is a continuous process rather than a one-time shot. Use these tools when possible and continually improve your design and testing strategy.

A: The Pex tests you are generating would ensure that your ArrayList's Count is consistent, regardless of the Contains method you are testing.
However, keep in mind that a single Pex run can only prove to the best of your knowledge that your code is consistent at the moment it is run. The tests it performs do not prove the code is correct for all possible inputs/states. 
For example, Pex tests don’t prove that your ArrayList will retain its order when items are deleted, only that there are no incorrect ordering issues in your current state of the ArrayList.
So in conclusion, although the Pex tests provide some level of confidence in terms of consistent behavior, they should be used in conjunction with a comprehensive suite of unit tests to truly test your code’s functionality and behavior comprehensively.
Remember, the purpose of automated tests is to catch problems in the development phase, not to prove that your code is correct for all possible inputs/state transitions.
This is a known problem in Pex, but this is one aspect of unit testing where tools like Pex can greatly improve the process. But more importantly, it encourages comprehensive testing strategies.

A: ArrayList's Contains method doesn't ensure elements aren't out of order, just that it contains the element in question. This implies that it doesn't care about the order of a list because its operations (Add and Remove) are based on reference comparison rather than value comparison. So, if elements are not in order, but you assert that the list Contains an element, it will still return true because the element is in the list based on reference comparison not value comparison.
Pex's test you create doesn't necessarily prove your code is 'correct' for all possible inputs/state transitions, it simply tests for 'consistencies in the operations'. Here the Contains method is a part of this test scenario.
For comprehensive testing, you should also test for--------------------            Debug.Assert( Old_arrListIndexOfX >=0 && ( (New_Ret == false && New_arrListIndexOfX < Old_arrListIndexOfX ) ||(New_Ret == true  && New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX )) );

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is false, last index of x before is less than or equal after
            Debug.Assert( Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >=0 && ( (New_Ret == false && New_arrListLastIndexOfX > Old_arrListLastIndexOfX ) ||(New_Ret == true  && New_arrListLastOfX <= Old_arrListLastOfX )) );

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret== arrList.Contains(x));
        }
    }
}
    
```

The PexAsmble methods are used to specify any pre/postconditions, or invariants, you want to keep true. The method is assumed under test so you can perform any operations using the initial state of the object.

In this case we have validated the Contains method from the ArrayList class in the NET Framework, observing the preconditions before the operation and after the operation for various properties of ArrayList such as Count, and IndexOf, LastIndexOf.

After observing the states, validating the invariants ensuring the preconditions are not violated and the properties of the underlying Objects or their behaviour does not change post the operation. 

Also as part of the test coverage, we are checking that if the Contains method returned false, IndexOf of the element and the LastIndexOf of the element should have either decreased or stayed the same respectively. 
By running the above program, Pex is able to generate a large set of test cases automatically which includes the various edge cases, and can fully validate your contract in all these states.

## PexInstrumentation
In Pex, Instrumentation is a technique of adding PexStress annotations to the original code. These annotations allow Pex to analyze the code and understand how different parameters might affect the outcome.

Instrumenting means to add special annotations to the source code to inform the Pex engine about the properties and constraints associated with the code under test. For instance, a parameter cannot be null or a return value has a specific range.

```csharp
[PexMethod]
public int Sum(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
 }
```

To instrument this as follows ...

```csharp
[PexMethod]
public int Sum([PexAssumeRange(1, 10)] int a, [PexAssumeRange(1, 10)] int b)
{
    return a + b;
}
```

In the above example, Pex will not generate any tests for the parameter a and b if the range is not within [1,10] since we added "[PexAssumeRange(1, 10)]" annotation.

## PexSettings
There are numerous settings that are available for Pex to configure the behavior of the engine. These settings can be done using `PexSettings` classes. 

Here are some important settings that we can set:

* `MaxConstraintSolverTime`: This decides the maximum time in milliseconds that Pex should spend trying to generate valid inputs.
* `MaxBranches`: This decides the maximum number of branches for which Pex should explore.
* `MaxClauses`: This decides the maximum number of clauses for which Pex should explore in every branch.
* `ForceHighMaxConstraints`: This enables Pex to generate tests that use higher maximum number of clauses and constraints.
* `StrictMode`: This is used for a very rigorous mode where Pex expects to solve the constraints successfully at every test generation.
* `ReflectiveSearchLimitMilliseconds`: This decides the maximum number of milliseconds Pex should spend on the reflective search.

Here's an example of setting various Pex settings:

```csharp
[PexMethod]
[PexSettings(MaxConstraintSolverTime = 1500, MaxBranches = 50, MaxClauses = 50)]
public int TestMethod(int x, string s) { ... }
```

In the above example, the maximum constraint solving time is set to 1500ms, the maximum number of branches set to 50 and the maximum number of clauses set to 50--------------------            if (New_Ret)  
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 );
            }
            else
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX < 0);
            }

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" That if the return value is true, index of x is same before and after
            if(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0)  
            {
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
            }

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" That if the return value is true, last index of x is same or increasing after the add
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);	
        }

    }
}
```

##### Remarks
This testing strategy is about verifying that the array list methods provide the correct results, based on the contract assertions. Here, we are testing the "arrayList.Contains(x)" method, which should return "TRUE" if x is found in the list and "FALSE" otherwise.

We first observe the old values of "Count", "IndexOf(x)" and "LastIndexOf(x)" before executing the method. Then we call "Contains(x)", observe the new values and check the results again the old and new values.

We perform three assertions to ensure the correctness of the results:

1. If "Contains(x)" returns true, the index of "x" is always 0 or more. The old value of IndexOf(x) can't be negative.

2. In case "Contains(x)" returns true, the index of "x" should be the same before and after the method execution.

3. After adding x to the list with "Add(x)" and checking "Contains(x)", the "LastIndexOf(x)" should be the same or greater than before.

This approach will help prevent errors in further code executions that may follow the method being tested.

#### 6.5 Pairwise Testing

Pairwise testing is a comprehensive method for testing a program. The idea is simple - from a test matrix, every possible combination of inputs are tested with a pair of values.

A 2x2 matrix would have tests for 4 different outcomes. A 3x3 matrix would have tests for 9 different outcomes. In general, the number of tests needed for a matrix with "n" inputs would be "n^2".

This approach is highly useful in testing all possible combinations of test cases. It's also effective at spotting defects that come from combinations of inputs.

#### 6.6 White-box and Black-box Testing

White-box testing is the testing of an application based on its internal structures (i.e., how code is written). This kind of testing is done by testers who know the internal workings of the code. Black-box testing, on the other hand, is the practice of testing an application based on its required functions without any prior knowledge of its internal structure.

In summary, the key differences between the two methods lie in how rigorous they are and in which aspects they emphasize. White-box testing is mostly used for automated systems, while black-box testing tends to involve manual tasks and tests.

#### 6.7 Software Testing Techniques & Tools

A few popular tools used in Software Testing are:

1. Selenium: A popular open-source tool that allows automating browsers.
2. SoapUI: An universal tool for WebServices testing.
3. JMeter: An open-source, Java-based tool for Performance Testing.
4. Postman: A popular API Testing Tool which allows the users to perform all the HTTP request types.
5. Mocha/Chai: Frameworks for test automation.
6. TestComplete: A complete suite of tools for software testing.
7. Fiddler: A web debugging tool that lets you monitor all HTTP(S) traffic between your computer and the Internet.

Auxiliary Testing Tools/Techniques:

1. Test Driven Development (TDD)
2. Behavior Driven Development (BDD)
3. Pair Programming
4. Continuous Integration (CI)
5. Software Configuration Management (SCM) tools
6. Automated Testing Tools
7. Load Testing Tools.

